{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Twenty-Five.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 2.009,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Teaspoon Thief.",
   "start": 2.609,
   "end": 3.867,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "For three mornings in a row, a silver teaspoon went missing from the Kettle and Gull.",
   "start": 5.267,
   "end": 9.897,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Every night, Agnes Bell lays out the tables for breakfast and leaves a tray of clean spoons on the sideboard under the back window. Then she locks both doors, and keeps the only keys in her apron pocket. The back window stays open at the top, just a hand's width, to let the kitchen air out. Nobody could squeeze through a gap that size, not even a small child.",
   "start": 10.747,
   "end": 33.339,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Monday morning, one silver teaspoon was gone from the tray. On Tuesday, another. On Wednesday, a third, and also a scrap of shiny silver foil from a chocolate wrapper that Pip had left on the sideboard.",
   "start": 34.189,
   "end": 47.799,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Strangely, other things on the same sideboard were never touched. There was a jar of coins for the lifeboat. There were wooden spoons, and a dull old pewter spoon. There was a sugar bowl, and a whole plate of shortbread under a cloth. Only the shiny things went.",
   "start": 48.649,
   "end": 65.055,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes's three regular early helpers were all a little offended to be asked. Pip pointed out that he couldn't stand teaspoons and only ever used a big spoon. Mr Fenwick said he'd been at the grocer's from five each morning, which his delivery driver confirmed. Loveday said she had been at home in bed, and so had Pickle the terrier, and neither of them had keys.",
   "start": 65.905,
   "end": 87.921,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Wednesday, Agnes found something on the back windowsill, just under the gap at the top of the window. It was a long, glossy feather, black, with a patch of bright white near the end.",
   "start": 88.771,
   "end": 100.205,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who has been taking the teaspoons?",
   "start": 101.305,
   "end": 104.143,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 105.343,
   "end": 106.367,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 107.267,
   "end": 112.493,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 116.993,
   "end": 118.017,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "None of the suspects took the spoons. The thief was a magpie.",
   "start": 119.117,
   "end": 122.808,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The doors were locked every night and Agnes had the only keys. The only way in was the gap at the top of the back window, just a hand's width, far too small for any person. But it was plenty of room for a bird.",
   "start": 123.658,
   "end": 137.119,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The thief took only shiny things: silver spoons and a scrap of silver foil. It ignored the coins in the jar, the dull pewter spoon, the wooden spoons and even the shortbread. Magpies are famous for snatching small, bright, shiny objects. And on the windowsill, right under the gap, was a glossy black feather with a patch of white, which is exactly what a magpie feather looks like.",
   "start": 137.969,
   "end": 161.756,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Pip climbed the old apple tree behind the tea room, very carefully, and found a messy nest of twigs. In it were three silver teaspoons, a scrap of foil, two bottle tops, and a shiny button that Loveday had lost in the spring. Agnes now closes the back window at night and leaves a bottle top on the garden wall for the magpie, by way of a thank you for the excitement.",
   "start": 162.606,
   "end": 185.475,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
